ASoC: sunxi: h713-msp: rebuild after a sleep only with rebuild_after_sleep

The rebuild after a wake is the first access to the TVFE island after
genpd powered pd_tvfe off and on again. Whether the domain is really back
on is not measured: genpd marks it on even when the power-on times out,
and the island is known to hang the CPU without an abort. The rebuild
runs on every wake once the module is loaded, which it is through its
device tree alias, so it would run in every standby stage of the crust
release, not only in the one that checks the speaker. A hang there would
look like a standby fault in a stage that measures something else.

The new parameter rebuild_after_sleep (bool, 0644) is off by default.
Off, suspend and resume leave the island and the driver alone, as in the
standby measured so far, and the wake logs "the DSP is not rebuilt" if it
was running. On, suspend and resume do what they did before. The stage
that checks the speaker sets it, on the command line as
sun50i_h713_msp.rebuild_after_sleep=1 or in sysfs before the suspend.

Not measured. Compiled out of tree with W=1, no new warning.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/sound/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-msp.c
+++ b/sound/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-msp.c
@@ -356,6 +356,15 @@ static unsigned int graph = MSP_GRAPH_MIXED;
 module_param(graph, uint, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(graph, "DSP graph: 0 HDMI only (path 0xC1), 1 HDMI mixed with the bridge PCM (path 0x89, default), 2 mixed without SURRDEC/SURRPOSTPRO");
 
+/*
+ * Off until a standby run on the device has shown the island after a wake:
+ * the rebuild below is the first access to it, and whether pd_tvfe is back
+ * on by then is not measured (the island can hang the CPU without an abort).
+ */
+static bool rebuild_after_sleep;
+module_param(rebuild_after_sleep, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rebuild_after_sleep, "Rebuild the DSP after a system sleep (default off: the sleep leaves the island alone)");
+
 /*
  * The TVFE clock gates in the order dsp_island2.py turns them on
  * (TVFE_CLKS, CCU offsets 0xd10..0xd60). All of them, not just the three
@@ -464,7 +473,7 @@ struct h713_msp {
 
 	enum msp_state state;
 	unsigned int heal_count;
-	bool rebuild_on_resume;		/* lock: running or healing at suspend */
+	bool rebuild_on_resume;		/* set by suspend, taken by resume */
 	unsigned int mbox_timeouts;
 	unsigned int mbox_reissues;
 	unsigned int blocks;
@@ -2440,13 +2449,20 @@ static void msp_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
  * the noirq phase whatever the runtime reference above says: the DSP loses
  * its patch and its graph. HDMI audio does not pass the ARM, so nothing
  * would notice; the rebuild is started here as self-healing from the patch
- * kept in RAM, and runs outside the resume path.
+ * kept in RAM, and runs outside the resume path. Only with
+ * rebuild_after_sleep; without it the sleep leaves the island and the
+ * driver alone, and the wake says so.
  */
 static int msp_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct h713_msp *msp = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
-	bool pending = cancel_work_sync(&msp->heal);
+	bool pending;
+
+	msp->rebuild_on_resume = false;
+	if (!READ_ONCE(rebuild_after_sleep))
+		return 0;
 
+	pending = cancel_work_sync(&msp->heal);
 	mutex_lock(&msp->lock);
 	msp_pcm_island_lost(msp);
 	msp->rebuild_on_resume = pending || msp->state == MSP_STATE_RUNNING;
@@ -2465,6 +2481,8 @@ static int msp_resume(struct device *dev)
 		msp->heal_count = 0;
 		schedule_work(&msp->heal);
 		dev_info(dev, "standby: the DSP is rebuilt after the wake\n");
+	} else if (msp->state == MSP_STATE_RUNNING) {
+		dev_info(dev, "standby: the DSP is not rebuilt (rebuild_after_sleep=0), its state after the wake is not checked\n");
 	}
 	mutex_unlock(&msp->lock);
 	return 0;
